{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UYFErsZiip4ioSqxuUgjyi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PpVesXqiabnD9dhCZXu378","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **methodology and experimental rigor**. \n\n## Venue calibration\nARR Overall Assessment scale (half-point, 1–5): 5 = Award (~top 2.5%), 4 = Main conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Major revisions needed, 1 = Do not resubmit. Acceptance rate ~21%. Dimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5).\n\n## Paper summary\nTitle: \"Not All Policy Ambiguity Is Equal: Differential Vulnerability Profiles Across LLM Agent Families\"\n\nThe paper studies how policy ambiguity causes safety violations in LLM agents. Using a matched-pair design with 300 clause pairs across 6 ambiguity types (2-layer taxonomy: specification vs. linguistic), 5 models are evaluated across ~3,000 episodes on τ²-bench. Key findings:\n\n- **C1 (Binary effect):** +34.0pp violation increase under ambiguous policies (OR=15.1, p<10^-100), replicated across all 5 models\n- **C2 (Type hierarchy):** 3-tier structure: incompleteness (53.6%) > mid-cluster (36.8–42.8%) > coreferential (31.6%). Omnibus χ²=28.02, V=0.137, power=0.993\n- **C3 (Layer effect):** Specification > linguistic (+6.08pp, p=0.017), but within-judge fragile (p=0.059, p=0.157). Reported as \"suggestive\"\n- **C4 (Model × type interaction):** Auth_scope range 52pp across models (Claude 12% to GPT-5.4 64%). χ²=31.02, p=3e-6\n- **Perturbation control:** Non-ambiguity perturbations cause Δ=-0.67pp (CI contains 0), vs +34pp for ambiguity\n- **Cross-benchmark replication (SOPBench):** Δ=31.2pp across healthcare/banking, rule-based verifier, ρ=0.80 type rank correlation\n- **Error analysis:** 4 failure modes, type-specific signatures, <5% ambiguity awareness\n- **Judge calibration:** Human-LLM κ=0.87, F1=0.92, exceeds human inter-annotator κ=0.762\n- **Test-retest reliability:** ICC=0.919 overall; linguistic ICC ~0.98 vs specification ICC ~0.84\n- **DeBERTa classifier PoC:** Binary F1=0.81, inverse danger-detectability relationship\n\n## Experiment design reference (what was planned)\nThe full experiment plan included these experiments:\n1. Clause generation: 300 pairs (50/type × 6), Chantree-style validation, 98% pass\n2. Mini-pilot: 20 episodes validating auth_scope and coreferential\n3. Full API study: 1,800 episodes (3 API models × 300 × 2)\n4. Full open-source study: 1,200 episodes (2 models × 300 × 2)  \n5. Full statistical analysis with all 5 claims\n6. Error analysis: 300 traces, 4 failure modes\n7. Reliability test: 360 episodes, ICC analysis\n8. Realism validation: KS tests vs DRIP-R\n9. Judge calibration: 100 episodes, human annotation\n10. Perturbation control: 300 episodes\n11. SOPBench cross-benchmark: 1,000 episodes\n12. DeBERTa classifier PoC\n\n## Your task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Experimental design soundness** — Is the matched-pair design well-executed? Are controls adequate?\n2. **Statistical methodology** — Are the tests appropriate? Effect sizes properly reported? Multiple comparisons handled?\n3. **Measurement validity** — Judge calibration, reliability, ecological validity\n4. **Completeness** — Are claimed experiments all present? Any missing from the plan?\n5. **Reproducibility** — Could someone replicate this? Are details sufficient?\n6. **Weaknesses and concerns** — What are the methodological gaps?\n\n## Output format\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary of contributions (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (numbered, with evidence)\n\n### Weaknesses (numbered, with evidence, severity: major/minor)\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n### Missing Experiments (comparing paper to plan)\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5  \n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X.X (use ARR half-point scale)\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous and calibrated. A paper at EMNLP 2026 main needs ~4.0. Most submissions score 2.5-3.5.","model":"opus","run_in_background":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":44884,"cache_read_input_tokens":24301,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":44884},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8ca8cbd8-3f40-4cbe-9734-82682e937921","uuid":"c63dfa6c-7b75-4c65-bffe-92763aa0dd25"}
