{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UYFErsZiip4ioSqxuUgjyi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MB54bVBdpBMX5EPy6eShhG","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\n## Venue calibration\nARR Overall Assessment scale (half-point, 1–5): 5 = Award (~top 2.5%), 4 = Main conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Major revisions needed, 1 = Do not resubmit. Acceptance rate ~21%. Dimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5).\n\n## Paper summary\nTitle: \"Not All Policy Ambiguity Is Equal: Differential Vulnerability Profiles Across LLM Agent Families\"\n\nThe paper is the first causal, type-granular, cross-model study of how policy ambiguity drives safety violations in LLM agents. Key elements:\n\n**Taxonomy:** Two-layer (specification vs. linguistic), six-type ambiguity taxonomy grounded in Berry & Kamsties (requirements engineering) and Li et al. (NLP ambiguity). Specification layer: authorization scope, incompleteness, conditional precedence. Linguistic layer: scopal, lexical, coreferential.\n\n**Design:** Matched-pair design with 300 clause pairs, perturbation control (synonym substitution + clause reordering), 5 models (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Claude Sonnet 4.6, Qwen3-235B, DeepSeek-V3), ~3,000 episodes on τ²-bench.\n\n**Claims:**\n- C1: Universal binary effect (+34.0pp, OR=15.1)\n- C2: Three-tier type hierarchy (V=0.137, power=0.993)\n- C3: Layer effect (suggestive, aggregate p=0.017 but within-judge fragile)\n- C4: Model × type interaction (52pp auth_scope range, χ²=31.02)\n- C5: Type-specific failure modes + ambiguity blindness (<5% awareness)\n\n**Theoretical contributions:**\n- \"Differential nocuity\" framework extending Berry & Kamsties\n- \"Systematic nocuity\" (27% convergence on same wrong interpretation)\n- \"Disambiguation channel framework\" (external channels explain layer hierarchy)\n- \"Ambiguity blindness\" concept\n\n**Cross-benchmark:** SOPBench replication across healthcare/banking (Δ=31.2pp, rule-based verifier)\n\n**Related work cited:** Berry & Kamsties, Massey et al., Chantree et al., Ferrari et al., Li et al., DRIP-R, τ²-bench, SOPBench, ToolEmu, R-Judge, InjecAgent, PolicyBank, IFEval, ToolBench, prompt sensitivity studies, garden-path effects in LLMs, Poesio & Artstein, Lassiter & Goodman.\n\n## Your task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment** — How new are the research questions, methods, and findings? Is \"differential nocuity\" a genuine conceptual advance or rebranding?\n2. **Related work coverage** — Are key works cited? Any major gaps?\n3. **Positioning** — Is the paper well-situated in the literature? Are claims of \"first\" justified?\n4. **Theoretical contributions** — Are the frameworks (differential nocuity, disambiguation channels, systematic nocuity) well-grounded and useful?\n5. **Significance and impact** — Will this influence future work? Is the community interest sufficient for EMNLP?\n6. **Writing quality** — Is the paper clearly written? Well-organized?\n7. **References** — The paper has many ?? placeholders in citations. Note this.\n\n## Output format\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary of contributions (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (numbered, with evidence)\n\n### Weaknesses (numbered, with evidence, severity: major/minor)\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n### Missing Related Work\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X.X (use ARR half-point scale)\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous and calibrated. A paper at EMNLP 2026 main needs ~4.0. Most submissions score 2.5-3.5.","model":"opus","run_in_background":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creati