orks (differential nocuity, disambiguation channels, systematic nocuity) well-grounded and useful?\n5. **Significance and impact** — Will this influence future work? Is the community interest sufficient for EMNLP?\n6. **Writing quality** — Is the paper clearly written? Well-organized?\n7. **References** — The paper has many ?? placeholders in citations. Note this.\n\n## Output format\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary of contributions (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (numbered, with evidence)\n\n### Weaknesses (numbered, with evidence, severity: major/minor)\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n### Missing Related Work\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall Assessment: X.X (use ARR half-point scale)\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous and calibrated. A paper at EMNLP 2026 main needs ~4.0. Most submissions score 2.5-3.5.","outputFile":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-ubuntu--agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0519-4-projects-policy-ambiguity-safety/8ca8cbd8-3f40-4cbe-9734-82682e937921/tasks/aa6055518e30c30d9.output","canReadOutputFile":true}}
